[Music]  the UK is already feeling the effects of  climate change may have been an  afterthought at the last election but  not anymore  this must be the climate election the  future won't get another chance a new  industrial revolution a green industrial  revolution now there are protests in the  street and real political friction over  what to do you can have targets till the  cows come home we're doing an enormous  amount but we need to clearly to do more  so are the parties being straightforward  about their plans we are going to have  to spend this money as a country the  question is not that should we spend it  the question is when and are the British  people ready to go green we have to  create an urgency are there prepared  I hope so we need to get it right  [Music]  the road to Fish Lake Doncaster we're  just a few weeks ago a month's worth of  rain fell in a day for Psalm it's been a  glimpse of a future shaped by extreme  weather events made worse by global  warming climate change when it happens  in your own backyard when it happens in  your own sleepy village you do sit up  and take more of a notice it hits home  it's very very frightening the thought  of this starting to happen more regular  volunteer Wendy Brown bridge has come to  the same conclusion suppose she could  have said have been a bit complacent my  husband was born in this village and  they always said it never flood not  anymore  their concern reflects a national trend  a sky data survey found that more than  half of people believe climate change  now poses a major threat to the UK as  unusual as it may sound in the current  political era the issue of tackling  climate change which means getting to  net zero carbon emissions in the coming  decades it's one of the things that all  the main parties actually agree on but  there the consensus ends there are huge  differences on how to do that how far  and how fast to go and how much money  it's going to take it's the realization  that what's going on in Venice and  Australia and the Arctic fires that's  going on the north of England these are  extreme weather events this is climate  breakdown this is what it looks like the  party with the most uncompromising views  on what to do rather unsurprisingly  is the Green Party Jonathan Bartley  wants to get to net zero carbon  emissions by 2030 20 years before the  government's own target the UK needs to  transform it needs to completely  transform we need to decarbonize every  sector of the economy agriculture  industry energy transport and how much  money is that going to cost we've said  our green New Deal we've been very  upfront about it will cost 91 billion a  year in terms of borrowing and another 9  billion for the running costs in through  increasing corporation tax from 18% to  24% that is a massive increase in  government borrowing it is very  significant increase in government  borrowing but we know that the climate  emergency in climate breakdown will cost  us far more in the long run we are going  to have to spend this money as a country  the question is not should we spend it  the question is when others say it's  irresponsible and will plunge this  country into irrecoverable debt what do  you say to those accusations  I'd say where's the hope at the end of  the second world war we were more in  debt as a country as a proportion  GDP than we are today yet we set up the  NHS we set up the welfare state we  established a new social contract with  people we revolutionized the way our  country lived we can do the same thing  today there is a perception that  actually it's easy for you to promise  these things because the reality is  you're never going to be in power is  that true  time and time and time again we've set  the agenda we've been on the right side  of history where Greens lead others have  followed and I believe on this they will  also have to follow there is no choice  [Music]  labour is promising a similar radical  big-spending approach a green industrial  revolution targeting energy production  jobs transport and more labour seems to  be promising an awful lot here massive  investment hundreds of thousands of new  well-paid jobs billions in economic  benefits and no one left behind it's  wishful thinking that you can deliver  all of that isn't it it's not wishful  thinking because we've got the credible  plans to back it all if we don't take  this action not only will we be heading  towards climate catastrophe but we'll  have missed the biggest opportunity we  have seen in generations to transform  our economy and usher in the next  industrial revolution the revolution  includes an ambitious plan to retrofit  nearly every UK home with energy-saving  measures like insulation better windows  and heating systems labour hopes grants  and interest-free loans will help  talking about upgrading 27 million her  yes over 10 years rough maths is that  you have to do 7,000 homes a day  approximately that's the rate at which  you have to upgrade homes every day for  the next ten that's right but that will  if you take a phased approach to it and  you concentrate predominantly at the  start to those homes that are in the  most need whilst you're building up your  skills base while you're allowing  businesses to invest how are you going  to convince homeowners and households to  get all of this done well certainly what  I'd say is we're not going to force any  homeowner to have changes made to their  home  they don't want to it's all their own  personal choice what we think that by  explaining the scheme to them showing  them that their energy bills are going  to come down that it would be a  no-brainer  this seems optimistic we did the work in  2014  it took about one summer Rob Jones has  already added installation triple  glazing even solar panels it has his  doubts about this happening everywhere  this kind of national program hasn't  really been seen since maybe the war I  don't think any of the parties are  necessarily particularly serious about  how this is going to be accomplished  I can't underestimate the amount of work  that took place in this house  replicating that for tens of millions of  households across Britain is a serious  task I'm not sure many of the parties  are very honest with the public about  about the impact of that or about the  fact that there isn't much of a choice  arguably but there are other issues at  play than just practicality in the  northwest for example the attitude to  going green is not a straightforward one  Manchester's mills and factories put  this region at the center of the  industrial revolution which helped  kickstart global warming and it's voters  have plenty to say about potentially  being on the cusp of another great  change whatever the rival parties say  voters know that transformation on this  scale means sacrifice  we're in a position where money seems to  be needed in every area of our country  NHS education you name it we need money  in the to be invested in the  infrastructure but I guess it's  something that we seriously have to  think about because without a world to  live in safely and you know what future  have we got anyway the next challenge is  grasping what that future might mean  especially for those already struggling  to get ahead  is there any party that you would  consider voting for on the basis of  their climate policies  yeah labour do you worry that as a  steelworker  which is obviously quite carbon  intensive if we have a green industrial  revolution you might be left behind  [Music]  maybe there are others there's a  lingering sense that tackling climate  change is an unfair attack on a precious  heritage without manchester in the 1860  and 1870s  would the world be where it is now why  should we be punished as our little  nation fuel our cars for what's  happening now in the world when you've  got the likes of china and russia and  they come to our country say oh you  can't have a light bulb on for more than  seven minutes because it's blue in there  there are signs that climate change may  emerge as a new political fault line in  this country 58% of those who voted  labour at the last election said it  mattered a great deal that the party  they support makes tackling the issue a  priority nearly double the number who  voted conservative and of those who  wanted to remain in europe it was over  half who felt the same way versus just  over a quarter of leaf voters for the  conservatives who are running on a  brexit platform this means navigating  fraught territory isn't the reality that  your party has made this election about  brexit and therefore it cannot really  afford to fully concentrate and deliver  on climate change pledges I think it's  the opposite I think the only way we're  going to get government focused properly  or Parliament focused properly on this  Jagan  antek challenge of the environment and  climate is by getting grexit done  otherwise we're going to have a  continued state of paralysis the Prime  Minister has declared vote blue go green  on a number of occasions but when you  dig into it the criticism is the party's  record just doesn't stand up to that  there's been a moratorium but not a ban  on fracking you frozen fuel duty you've  banned onshore wind you've ended  subsidies for solar panels there's been  plenty of spending on new road building  just let me finish this there's going to  be a third runway at Heathrow so isn't  it the case that actually when it comes  to tackling climate change your party is  all talking their action I won't pretend  that any government anywhere in the  world is doing enough at the scale of  the challenge I mean if the science is  right and I believe it is then we face  an emergency and I don't think the  collective response of governments  around the world including ours has been  enough and I would never pretend it is I  think we need to continuously do more  the other parties are already promising  to do so all of them suggesting they can  go further and faster are you being out  greened I think from the government's  point of view 2050 is the right target I  actually think we could get there sooner  but I don't think we should legislate  for it to happen sooner but we just  don't know if that's possible  like the Conservatives the Lib Dems have  been accused of not being ambitious  enough on climate they are deeply  concerned about bringing people with  them proposing citizens assemblies to  help unless we get individuals on board  with the difficult transition we need to  make and there needs to be a fair  transition we might get backlash like we  have seen with religion and in France  and we absolutely need to avoid that  because that must stop progress other  British people ready to make all the  changes you've been talking about we  have to create that urgency are they're  prepared I hope so we need to get it  right there's no question that we need  to do this and I'm absolutely committed  to make sure that this doesn't end up in  a party political dingdong this is has  to become something that we embrace as  people together or we are finished but  it's too late to take the politics out  of dealing with climate change  now it's on the ballot paper voters face  a defining choice about what happens to  this country in our warming world Hanna  Thomas Peter Sky News

